content.text:
It's the native browser **View Transitions API**, not a from-scratch JS animation. I pulled the actual site source to confirm the mechanism:

**JS (on toggle click):**
```js
onClick: (e) => {
  const dark = !document.documentElement.classList.contains("dark");
  const rect = e.currentTarget.getBoundingClientRect();
  const x = rect.left + rect.width / 2, y = rect.top + rect.height / 2;
  const r = Math.hypot(Math.max(x, innerWidth - x), Math.max(y, innerHeight - y)) + 180;

  document.documentElement.style.setProperty("--reveal-x", `${x}px`);
  document.documentElement.style.setProperty("--reveal-y", `${y}px`);
  document.documentElement.style.setProperty("--reveal-r", `${r}px`);

  document.startViewTransition(() => {
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle("dark", dark);
  });
}
```
It just records the click position and the max radius needed to cover the screen, then flips the `dark` class inside `document.startViewTransition()`.

**CSS (does the actual animating):**
```css
::view-transition-new(root) {
  mask-image: radial-gradient(circle at var(--reveal-x) var(--reveal-y),
    #000 calc(var(--reveal-progress) - 160px), transparent var(--reveal-progress));
  animation: theme-reveal .4s cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1) forwards;
}

@keyframes theme-reveal {
  0%  { --reveal-progress: 0px; }
  to  { --reveal-progress: var(--reveal-r); }
}
```
The browser snapshots old/new page states automatically; this just masks the "new" (post-toggle) snapshot with a radial gradient whose radius grows from the click point, producing the circular wipe. It also respects `prefers-reduced-motion` and falls back to an instant class swap if the API isn't supported or `startViewTransition` doesn't exist (Firefox).

Since your site (`Layout.astro`) already has a theme-toggle button doing an instant class swap, I could add this same circular-reveal effect — it's maybe 15 lines of CSS + a small tweak to the existing click handler. Want me to wire it in?

